Not any more...but not that long ago, they refused to include many programs
built for KDE because of a licensing technicality. They claimed that
distributing the programs violated their license due to an interaction with
the GPL, even though the programs' authors clearly intended those programs
to be distributed in that form, and even though Debian was the only
distribution that had this problem. They would only distribute those
programs that they'd obtained specific permission from the authors to do so.
They imposed this requirement on no other software. I don't recall what the
resolution was.

Personally, no matter what their protestations, I believe this was a naked
attempt to promote GNOME over KDE. After all, GNOME is more GNUically
correct, something the Debian project holds supremely dear.


